Q: Can you redefine the type of an object after the __init__ in python? A: <p>Short answer: you cannot do that at <code>__init__</code> level.</p>
<p>Medium answer: The Pythonic way is probably to create a <em>factory function</em> as explained in <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/79898048">David's answer</a></p>
<p>Longer answer: Python is kind enough to allow you a great level of customization. A class is normally in charge of creating its own instances, but is perfectly able to create objects from subclasses or even totally unrelated objects. But as this is rather <em>uncommon</em> you should ask yourself if you really want to go that way. In real world programming, that could cause headaches to peer-reviewers and they would send gripes at you.</p>
<p>The key is to use the <code>__new__</code> special method: it receives all the parameters and has to return an object - absolutely <em>any</em> object. The common way is to delegate the actual creation to <code>super().__new__</code> or <code>object.__new__</code>.</p>
<p>The hard part here if to make the link between the <code>'hoe'</code> string and the <code>Hoe</code> class. A simple dictionary could help here.</p>
<p>You would get:</p>
<pre class="lang-py prettyprint-override"><code>class Item:
    def __new__(cls, name, amount):
        realcls = _Item_subcls[name]
        # ensure that we got a true subclass...
        if not issubclass(realcls, cls):
            raise ValueError(f'{name} is not a valid Item name')
        return  super().__new__(realcls)

    # __init__ will be called here because we ensured that
    #  __new__ could only return instances of (subclasses of) Item
    def __init__(self, name, amount):
        self.name = name
        self.amount = amount

    def on_right_click(self):
        pass


class Hoe(Item):
    def on_right_click(self):
        self.do_something()

    def do_something(self):
        print(f'on_right_click called on Hoe {self.amount}')


_Item_subcls = {'hoe': Hoe}
</code></pre>
<p>This will work per your requirements, but I still urge you to wonder whether you could not use a different design to avoid that. A function may absolutely return an instance of a subclass, that having a <em>constructor</em> do that is at least a suspect design.</p>

Q: Set tuple values, inferring type from const array of std::variant A: <p>Here is another proposal that might be more idiomatic than using <a href="https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/utility/variant/index" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>std::variant::index</code></a>. In order to make the following more interesting, we will add another type to the variant, i.e. <code>std::variant&lt;int, std::string, double&gt;</code>.</p>
<p>Actually, when using <code>std::variant</code>, the idiomatic way to recover the actual type of the variant value is to use <a href="https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/utility/variant/visit" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>std::variant::visit</code></a>. By doing so, you have to provide one visitor whose parameter can handle each type of the variant. For instance, we could write (see <a href="https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/utility/variant/visit" rel="nofollow noreferrer">here</a> for <code>overloads</code> definition)</p>
<pre><code>auto visitor = overloads  {
   [&amp;](int)           {  std::get&lt;int&gt;        (tup) = 1;   },
   [&amp;](std::string s) {  std::get&lt;std::string&gt;(tup) = &quot;2&quot;; },
   [&amp;](double)        {  std::get&lt;double&gt;     (tup) = 3.0; }
};
std::visit (visitor, this-&gt;flavours[index]);
</code></pre>
<p>Note how we use here the possibility for <code>std::get</code> to use a type as template parameter instead of an integer index (possible when the tuple holds distinct types).</p>
<p>Unfortunately, this wouldn't compile in case <code>AllArgs...</code> holds only one type , say <code>int</code> for instance. In such a case, the <code>visitor</code> object (that must provide a case for each possible type) would try to access the tuple item for type <code>double</code> for instance, item that would not exist in that case.</p>
<p>In order to handle this, we begin by defining our visitor by</p>
<pre><code>std::visit ([&amp;] (auto const&amp; e) {
    (*this) (sausage_roll, order, e);
}, this-&gt;flavours[index]);
</code></pre>
<p>In other words, our visitor takes an <code>auto</code> object as input and forwards the
actual work to a functor; note that this functor takes <code>sausage_roll</code>, <code>order</code> and <code>e</code> (that can be of type <code>int</code>, <code>std::string</code> or <code>double</code>).</p>
<p>Now the problem becomes how to define the required overloads of this functor. First, we have to provide in <code>Greggs</code> an implementation that will do nothing when the type will not be one of the type in <code>AllArgs...</code>. This can be done with some SFINAE and a fold expression:</p>
<pre><code>template&lt;typename T, std::enable_if_t&lt;(not std::is_same_v&lt;T,AllArgs&gt; &amp;&amp; ...)&gt;* = nullptr&gt;
auto operator() (mytuple_t&amp; tup, std::string const&amp; order,  T n) const {  throw; }
</code></pre>
<p>Next, we have to provide a version of the functor for each type in <code>AllArgs...</code>. We can achieve this with template specialization. We begin by defining a generic case</p>
<pre><code>template&lt;typename T&gt;
struct SetTuple;
</code></pre>
<p>then the different specializations, one per possible type in the variant:</p>
<pre><code>template&lt;&gt;
struct SetTuple&lt;int&gt;  {
    template&lt;typename...Args&gt;
    auto operator() (std::tuple&lt;Args...&gt;&amp; tup, std::string const&amp; order, int) const {
        std::get&lt;int&gt;(tup) = std::stoi(order);
    }
};

template&lt;&gt;
struct SetTuple&lt;std::string&gt; {
    template&lt;typename...Args&gt;
    auto operator() (std::tuple&lt;Args...&gt;&amp; tup, std::string const&amp; order, std::string const&amp;) const {
        std::get&lt;std::string&gt;(tup) = order;
    }
};

template&lt;&gt;
struct SetTuple&lt;double&gt; {
    template&lt;typename...Args&gt;
    auto operator() (std::tuple&lt;Args...&gt;&amp; tup, std::string const&amp; order, double const&amp;) const {
        std::get&lt;double&gt;(tup) = std::stod(order);
    }
};
</code></pre>
<p>An interesting point here is that the third parameter acts like a tag that allows to distinguish the different overloads; we actually don't need the value of the argument.</p>
<p>We can finish the <code>Greggs</code> definition with some mixins mechanism, by inheriting from <code>SetTuple&lt;T&gt;</code> with <code>T</code> being one of the type in <code>ArgAlls...</code>. The final step is to have access to each inherited <code>operator()</code> within <code>Greggs</code> which can be done with <code>using</code>.</p>
<p>In the end, the whole code would be</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;array&gt;
#include &lt;string&gt;
#include &lt;tuple&gt;
#include &lt;variant&gt;
#include &lt;fmt/ranges.h&gt;

template&lt;typename T&gt;
struct SetTuple;

template&lt;&gt;
struct SetTuple&lt;int&gt;  {
    template&lt;typename...Args&gt;
    auto operator() (std::tuple&lt;Args...&gt;&amp; tup, std::string const&amp; order, int) const {
        std::get&lt;int&gt;(tup) = std::stoi(order);
    }
};

template&lt;&gt;
struct SetTuple&lt;std::string&gt; {
    template&lt;typename...Args&gt;
    auto operator() (std::tuple&lt;Args...&gt;&amp; tup, std::string const&amp; order, std::string const&amp;) const {
        std::get&lt;std::string&gt;(tup) = order;
    }
};

template&lt;&gt;
struct SetTuple&lt;double&gt; {
    template&lt;typename...Args&gt;
    auto operator() (std::tuple&lt;Args...&gt;&amp; tup, std::string const&amp; order, double const&amp;) const {
        std::get&lt;double&gt;(tup) = std::stod(order);
    }
};

using myvarian_t = std::variant&lt;int, std::string, double&gt;;

template&lt;typename... AllArgs&gt;
struct Greggs : SetTuple&lt;AllArgs&gt;...
{
    using SetTuple&lt;AllArgs&gt;::operator()...;

    using mytuple_t  = std::tuple&lt;AllArgs...&gt;;

    constexpr Greggs(AllArgs... args) : flavours({args...}) {}

    bool makeOrder(std::string const&amp; order, size_t index) {

        if (index &gt;= this-&gt;flavours.size())  return false;

        std::visit ([&amp;] (auto const&amp; e) {
            (*this) (sausage_roll, order, e);
        }, this-&gt;flavours[index]);

        fmt::println (&quot;sausage_roll: {}&quot;, sausage_roll);

        return true;
    }

    template&lt;typename T, std::enable_if_t&lt;(not std::is_same_v&lt;T,AllArgs&gt; &amp;&amp; ...)&gt;* = nullptr&gt;
    auto operator() (mytuple_t&amp; tup, std::string const&amp; order,  T n) const {  throw; }

    mytuple_t sausage_roll;
    std::array&lt;myvarian_t, sizeof...(AllArgs)&gt; flavours;
};

int main()
{
    Greggs(0, std::string(&quot;&quot;)).makeOrder(&quot;Vegan&quot;, 1);

    Greggs(0).makeOrder(&quot;1&quot;, 0);

    Greggs(std::string(&quot;&quot;)).makeOrder(&quot;73&quot;, 0);

    Greggs(0, std::string(&quot;&quot;), 0.0).makeOrder(&quot;3.14&quot;, 2);

    return 0;
}
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://godbolt.org/z/9Kf8vjG5T" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Demo</a></p>
<p>Note the usage of the <code>fmt</code> library that allows to directly dump (in C++17) the tuple content.</p>
<p>Possible output</p>
<pre><code>sausage_roll: (0, &quot;Vegan&quot;)
sausage_roll: (1)
sausage_roll: (&quot;73&quot;)
sausage_roll: (0, &quot;&quot;, 3.14)
</code></pre>
<hr />
<p><strong>Update</strong></p>
<p>A similar solution gathers all the overloads in a single struct <code>SetTupleValue</code>. It might be easier to use than template specializations since all the overloaded functions are at the same code location.</p>
<pre><code>template&lt;typename...AllArgs&gt;
struct SetTupleValue {

    std::tuple&lt;AllArgs...&gt;&amp; tup;
    std::string const&amp; order;

    template&lt;typename T&gt;
    struct fail_t {};

    template&lt;typename T&gt;
    using tag_t = std::conditional_t&lt;(std::is_same_v&lt;T,AllArgs&gt; || ...), T, fail_t&lt;T&gt;&gt;;

    template&lt;typename T&gt;  auto operator() (T)  {}

    auto operator() (tag_t&lt;int&gt;)         { std::get&lt;int&gt;(tup)         = std::stoi(order); }
    auto operator() (tag_t&lt;std::string&gt;) { std::get&lt;std::string&gt;(tup) = order; }
    auto operator() (tag_t&lt;double&gt;)      { std::get&lt;double&gt;(tup)      = std::stod(order); }
};
</code></pre>
<p>Here we use a <code>tag_t</code> struct that makes an overload visible to the overload resolution for a given type if this type belongs to the <code>AllArgs...</code> types. This can be used in <code>makeOrder</code> as follows:</p>
<pre><code>std::visit ([&amp;] (auto&amp;&amp; e) {
    SetTupleValue&lt;AllArgs...&gt;{sausage_roll,order} (e);
}, this-&gt;flavours[index]);
</code></pre>
<p>Here is a <a href="https://godbolt.org/z/nfscP91z6" rel="nofollow noreferrer">demo</a> with many possible types for the variant definition.</p>

Q: Checking the if current player has won the game of Tic Tac Toe in JavaScript A: <p>Two things: Use <code>innerHTML</code> to get the content of the div, not a string containing the whole div. Also check if there's actually 3 in a row, not just 2. (or you can move the <code>counter++;</code> to after the check, and leave it with <code>counter == 2</code>)</p>

<pre><code>if(cells[elem].innerHTML === curPlayer){
  counter++;
  if(counter == 3 ){
    winner = curPlayer;
    alert(winner);
  }
}
</code></pre>

<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>var player1 = "X";
var player2 = "O";
var cells = Array.from(document.querySelectorAll(".cell"));
var currentPlayer = player1;
var winner = "";
var winCombos = [[0,1,2],[3,4,5],[6,7,8],[0,3,6],[1,4,7],[2,5,8],[0,4,8],[2,4,6]];

function checkWinner(arr, curPlayer){
    arr.forEach(function(subArr){
      var counter = 0;
      subArr.forEach(function(elem){
        if(cells[elem].innerHTML === curPlayer){
          counter++;
          if(counter == 3 ){
            winner = curPlayer;
            alert(winner);
          }
        }
      });
    });
}

function move(e){
  if(e.target.className === "cell" &amp;&amp; e.target.textContent === ""){
    e.target.textContent = currentPlayer;
    checkWinner(winCombos, currentPlayer);
    currentPlayer = (currentPlayer === player1)?player2:player1;
  }
  
}

document.querySelector(".board").addEventListener("click", move);</code></pre>
